    "body": "### Problem\n\n**(btw, love the new multicursor feature 🎉!)**\n\nIn multicursor mode, the `%` motion only moves the \"active\" cursor to the matching brace. The other cursors don't move at all, even with follow mode on.\n\nOther motions seem to mirror fine -- `w` and `l` move all cursors in the repro below, it's just `%` that doesn't. So I think the builtin motions are only partially mirrored?\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `nvim -u NORC`\n2. Paste this into the buffer:\n    \n```lua\nkmap('i', '<C-l>', '<Right>', { noremap = true })\nkmap('i', '<C-h>', '<Left>', { noremap = true })\nkmap('i', 'jj', '<Esc>', { noremap = true })\n```\n\n3. Put a cursor on each `k` (`Q` over each one).\n4. Turn on follow mode with `q=`.\n5. `w` then `l` -- all three cursors are on `(` now. Good so far.\n6. Press `%`.\n\n[![asciicast](https://asciinema.org/a/pHIToY8by5fI2pjt.svg)](https://asciinema.org/a/pHIToY8by5fI2pjt)\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nAll three cursors jump to the matching `)`.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.13.0-dev-1474+gd42367570c\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno, they don't have multicursors\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nmacOS 26.6.2\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\ntmux-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nGitHub nightly download",
